## 2. Numbers: the one rule that cannot bend

Every number written into `thesis.tex` must meet one of two conditions:
- **(a)** it comes straight from an output of the figure script, `tests/analysis/thesis_figures.py`
  (part A.4 of `FIGURES_AND_STYLE.md` lists which numbers it produces);
- **(b)** you read it yourself, in this chat, from a raw result file under
  `/Volumes/seagate/kirby_results_2026-10-09/` (or a newer `kirby_results_*` folder if one
  exists; use the newest and say which).

Neither the outline, a findings file, an analysis page, memory nor an earlier chat counts as a
source. Each of them only tells you where to look.

- Put a LaTeX comment on the line above each number or group of numbers:
  `% SOURCE: <results folder>/<experiment>/<dataset>.json <key>, <n> folds, read 2026-10-..`.
  When the figure script is re-run, these comments are how the numbers get re-checked.
- The figure script has not been re-run on purpose: the author expects some of its output to
  change. Insert the figure and table environments pointing at its output paths. Write the
  captions. Do not run the script to make figures for the thesis unless the author asks.
- The analysis pages in `/Volumes/seagate/claude_analysis_2026-10-08/answers/` work as follows:
  - their tables were rebuilt from the 9 Oct results;
  - the hand-written summaries at the top of each page are stale;
  - another chat may be rewriting those pages now, so do not edit them.
- Say the fold count with every claim. Most noise results are outer fold 0 only. The complexity
  and similarity results mostly have 5 folds. `CH7_METHODS.md` §9 lists the exceptions.
- No significance tests, confidence intervals or p-values, across folds or across datasets.
  Report per-fold scores and their spread.
- Report numbers in the dataset's own metric first, and as raw pairs ("0.4930 to 0.4302 RMSE").
  A derived number (a percentage, a ratio) may follow, but never stands alone.
- Never write a bare count. Say what is counted: datasets, folds, molecules,
  model-and-configuration pairs.
- A result picked on the held-out fold is optimistic. Say so in the same sentence. One example:
  MEETING_METHODS.md picks each method's best setting on the held-out score.

## 3. What goes in the thesis and what does not

- **No patient data (2026-10-09).** TCGA, ANNITIA and NHANES never appear in any form, and
  their files are not opened. See the rule in `CLAUDE.md`. The outline was scrubbed on 2026-10-09;
  any count marked "[... patient dataset was removed ...]" there needs recounting.

- Experiment codes never appear in the thesis: not in text, captions, tables or figures. This
  covers c02, n18, s05, fed03 and every other code. Describe each experiment by what it does.
- Use the noise-method names exactly as set in the outline's "NAMES FOR THE NOISE METHODS":
  - detector and suspicion score;
  - noise mitigation, with three actions: delete, count less, move the label;
  - noise-robust training;
  - known flag, and every molecule.

  "LNL" is not a group name, and "LNL survey" never appears. "Repair", "cleanup", "correction"
  and "operation" are code words.
- Domain balance. There are 9 small-molecule datasets against 15 antibody ones, and no table,
  figure or paragraph may let antibodies dominate by count. Small molecules are the main focus.
  Antibody results that stand out get their own named discussion. Reactions and gene expression
  appear in smaller parts.
- A dataset with missing results is mentioned only if what it has shows something. Its missing
  results then go in the limitations.
- Tables:
  - follow the rules in the outline: a direction arrow on mixed-metric tables;
  - use selective bold, rule (a) or rule (b), and the caption says which;
  - put no bold on settings tables.
- Captions say only what is shown: rows, metric, unit and direction, in 2 to 4 sentences.
  Caveats go in the text.
- Never write an AI verdict into her documents as if it were measured. A reading by Claude is
  labelled as one.
- Do not cut a dataset, axis or method from the text to save effort. Something is left out only
  because it is degenerate, broken, undefined, or the author chose to leave it out.

## 4. Writing style: hers, not yours

- Keep her phrasing wherever it exists. `thesis.tex` Chapters 1-4 and the hybrid methods (5.2)
  are mostly her prose. `FIGURES_AND_STYLE.md` part B has her measured habits and 42 verbatim
  sentences. Read them before writing a line.
- In short:
  - American spelling;
  - "we";
  - a median sentence length of about 19 words, and no sentence over 30 words;
  - no em dashes;
  - no runs of short sentences;
  - plain verbs ("shows", "achieved", "produced");
  - no meta-commentary about a finding.
- Banned (`~/Documents/ai_phrasing.rtf`; all 15 rules are in part B.5):
  - "indicates that", "suggests that", "this pattern suggests", "demonstrates that" (use
    "shows that");
  - "emerged as", "warranted investigation", "the critical insight", "several X emerge",
    "actionable";
  - "read-across" and "SAC" anywhere.
- Methods are written from the code, in notation where a procedure is new. Do not narrate the
  code: no function names in prose, no "then ... next ... loops over". The test is: "would a
  reimplementer get a different result by guessing this differently?" Detail that fails the test
  goes in the appendix or nowhere.

## 5. Mechanics of editing `thesis.tex`

- There is one file: root `~/repos/KIRBy/thesis.tex` with root `refs.bib`. Never edit a stage1
  copy.
- **Every block of text you write gets `% TODO: manual review` on the line above it.** This
  covers paragraphs, captions, table rows and footnotes, with no exceptions. Use exactly that
  string. Older Claude blocks in the file say `% TODO: review, needs manual review`. Leave those
  as they are.
- From Chapter 5 onwards the existing text is out of date. Exceptions:
  - Chapter 5's hybrid methods (5.2) were recently rewritten;
  - Chapter 5's dataset and setup subsections are out of date.

  Replace out-of-date text; do not splice new sentences into it. Keep every `\label{}` that is
  referenced elsewhere. Grep for `\ref{<label>}` before removing or renaming one.
- Other chats may be editing `thesis.tex`. One is applying the noise-method names to Chapters 1
  to 4. Use small exact-string edits, re-read before each edit, and never rewrite the file
  wholesale.
- Back up before a large replacement. Copy the file to `thesis.tex.before_<what>_<date>` and
  look at the target before overwriting.
- Citations go in `refs.bib` only after the entry is checked against the publisher or Crossref.
  A key the outline marks "not verified" stays a `% TODO(cite)`.
- The generated tables need the `adjustbox` package, which root `main.tex` does not load.
  The script sizes figures for 6.5 in, but the text block is 5.91 in. Raise both with the author
  before relying on either; do not fix them silently.

## 6. How to work with the author

- The selection of results is hers. There are far more results than pages, and choosing them is
  hard. Before writing a results section:
  - list the candidate results with their numbers and fold counts;
  - recommend which to show;
  - ask her.

  Expect back and forth. Write after she answers.
- Bring decisions one per line, each with what changes either way and your recommendation.
  Never refer back to your own list by number.
- If what the results show goes against her expectation, say so plainly, with the numbers, in
  its own paragraph. Two examples are already known:
  - the antibody hypothesis (`CH8.md`, `PATTERNS.md`);
  - step 8 of the argument chain (`CH8.md`).
- Post a line every few minutes during long work. Her terminal disconnects.
